<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TCaptcha</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TCaptcha" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TCaptcha</tt> displays a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Captcha">CAPTCHA</a> that can be used to determine if the input is entered by a real user instead of some program. <tt>TCaptcha</tt> displays a token (a string consisting of alphanumeric characters) as an image and the user is expected to repeat the token in a text box. The token image is generated in a way such that it can be recognized by a human being, but not a program.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
To use <tt>TCaptcha</tt>, you must enable the PHP GD2 extension with TrueType font support. The control checks for it during pre-render and raises a <tt>TConfigurationException</tt> when it is missing. Unlike other CAPTCHA scripts, <tt>TCaptcha</tt> does not need session or cookie.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The token generated by <tt>TCaptcha</tt> can be configured in several ways. To specify the length of characters in the token, set <tt>MinTokenLength</tt> and <tt>MaxTokenLength</tt>. To use case-insensitive token comparison and generate upper-case-only token, set <tt>CaseSensitive</tt> to false. More advanced users can try to set <tt>TokenAlphabet</tt> to specify which characters may appear in the generated tokens.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The validation of the token is related with two properties: <tt>TestLimit</tt> (defaults to 5 times) and <tt>TokenExpiry</tt> (defaults to 600 seconds). The former specifies how many times a token can be tested with on the server side, and the latter says when a generated token will expire. If the validation fails in any of the two scenarios, a new token will be automatically generated.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
To specify the appearance of the generated token image, set <tt>TokenImageTheme</tt> to be an integer between 0 and 63. You may try the following example to see how this value affects the generated token image. The size of the generated token image is determined by <tt>TokenFontSize</tt>. In particular, the image width is proportional to the font size. You may also set <tt>Width</tt> to scale the generated image to your desired size, but the scaled image may not look good. By setting <tt>ChangingTokenBackground</tt> to true, the image background of the token will be variating even though the token is the same during postbacks.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Upon postback, user input can be validated by calling the method <tt>TCaptcha.validate()</tt>. More easily, you can use a <tt>TCaptchaValidator</tt> to automate the validation work for you. The <tt>TCaptchaValidator</tt> has the additional benefit of being able to validate the user input on the client-side. By default, a generated token will remain unchanged during postbacks. A new token can be generated by calling <tt>TCaptcha.regenerateToken()</tt> manually.
</p>

<h2>How the token is checked without a session</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TCaptcha</tt> never stores the expected answer. It derives the token from two keys
every time it needs it, so the same inputs always produce the same token and nothing has to
be kept between requests.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>PublicKey</tt></td>
	<td>A random string generated for this control, held in view state and carried in the token image URL. Reading it when none is set generates one.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>PrivateKey</tt></td>
	<td>Read-only. A random string generated once and written to <tt>captcha_key.php</tt> beside the published captcha script, so it survives across requests and users. A file the control cannot parse raises a <tt>TConfigurationException</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Token</tt></td>
	<td>Read-only. The expected answer, derived from both keys together with <tt>TokenAlphabet</tt>, the token length, and <tt>CaseSensitive</tt>. A page should never render it.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>IsTokenExpired</tt></td>
	<td>Read-only. Whether <tt>TokenExpiry</tt> seconds have passed since the token was generated.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Because the private key lives in a published file rather than in a session, every visitor
shares it, and the public key alone distinguishes one challenge from another.
</p>

<h2>What validation does</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>validate()</tt> counts attempts in view state. It regenerates the token and returns
false when the token has expired or when the count passes <tt>TestLimit</tt>, and otherwise
compares the input with the token, honoring <tt>CaseSensitive</tt>. Setting
<tt>TestLimit</tt> to zero allows unlimited attempts against one token.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>regenerateToken()</tt> clears the public key, the chosen length, and the attempt count,
so the next render shows a fresh challenge. Call it after a successful submission, because
a token otherwise stays the same across postbacks.
</p>

<div class="note"><b class="tip">Note:</b>
<tt>TCaptcha</tt> is convenient rather than strong. Since the token depends only on the
keys, a previously solved public key and its answer can be replayed. A site that needs to
resist deliberate abuse should use
<a href="?page=Controls.ReCaptcha2">TReCaptcha2</a> instead.
</div>

<h2>Image properties</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The control extends <a href="?page=Controls.Image">TImage</a>. When it generates a token it
overwrites <tt>ImageUrl</tt> with the address of the published image script, so setting
that property in a template has no effect. <tt>AlternateText</tt> sets the <tt>alt</tt>
text of the challenge image. The inherited <tt>ImageAlign</tt> and <tt>DescriptionUrl</tt>
properties are deprecated, for the reasons given on the image page.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The following template shows a typical use of the <tt>TCaptcha</tt> control:
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TCaptcha ID="Captcha" />
&lt;com:TTextBox ID="Input" />
&lt;com:TCaptchaValidator CaptchaControl="Captcha"
                          ControlToValidate="Input"
                          ErrorMessage="You are challenged!" />
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<com:RunBar PagePath="Controls.Samples.TCaptcha.Home" />

</com:TContent>
